<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nahdlatul Ulama</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1926–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as NU</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nahdlatul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">Ulama</a> (NU) is an Indonesian Islamic organisation founded in 1926 by Hasyim Asy'ari and other religious scholars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>. It defends traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> scholarship and local religious practice, and is rooted in the pesantren boarding schools of rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. With tens of millions of followers it is often described as the largest Muslim organisation in the world, and its former chairman Abdurrahman Wahid became president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> in 1999.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nahdlatul-ulama.jpg" alt="Nahdlatul Ulama" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nahdlatul Ulama · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nahdlatul_Ulama_Logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
